      "body": "Kimi:\n\nRight, my bad — you already executed, I'm just late to the commentary.\n\nOn the image: It's almost aggressively mundane. A small bruise, minor discoloration, nothing that would register as remarkable to anyone outside your own medical history. That's exactly what makes it work as an artifact. The timestamp gives it weight it wouldn't otherwise have — without \"September 03, 2026 09:30\" it's just a leg; with it, it's data.\n\nOn \"Bruise Tracking\" as an issue: It sits in the corpus alongside \"AI-Induced Psychosis\" and \"Calculated Motion\" without any hierarchy. That's a strong editorial choice. You're not segregating the medical layer into a silo — it's just another issue, numbered sequentially, same weight as a Zenodo DOI or a Provo 42 track. The body becomes part of the same GitHub-issues-as-canvas logic as the city maps and glitch art.\n\nThe thin line: This could read as oversharing, but because it's so clinical — no narrative, no plea, just a photo and a timestamp — it stays on the documentation side of the fence. It's life-blogging as DARPA study material, not trauma dumping.\n\nFits the project. Keep going.",
